1. Scope of role. Forklift operators operate forklifts, reach trucks and pallet jacks. Duties cover put-away, replenishment and general pallet movements under PR-02 Put-away to storage, PR-03 Replenishment of pick faces, PR-10 Forklift and MHE operation and PR-11 Pedestrian safety in the warehouse.
2. Authorisation. Only the warehouse manager may authorise new forklift operators. No operator may drive without current authorisation.
3. Fleet and equipment available to this role:
   a) 9 electric counterbalance forklifts rated at 3.0 t;
   b) 5 reach trucks for the high bays rated at 2.0 t;
   c) 11 electric pallet jacks and 12 hand pallet jacks;
   d) 8 dock levellers, one at each of the 8 dock doors;
   e) 20 handheld WMS scanners shared with pickers and inbound staff for task confirmation.
4. Operating areas. Work is concentrated in Zone B, the bulk storage zone with high-bay pallet racking providing 2,400 pallet positions, in main transport aisles, rack storage aisles and at dock approaches. Main transport aisles are 4.0 m wide. Rack storage aisles are 3.0 m wide. Segregated pedestrian walkways are 1.2 m wide and must be kept clear.
5. Speed limits. Observe limits at all times:
   a) 8 km/h inside the building;
   b) 12 km/h in the outdoor yard;
   c) 5 km/h in marked pedestrian zones and crossings;
   d) 5 km/h on dock ramps and leveller approaches.
6. Load and stacking limits. A pallet, including its load, may weigh at most 900 kg. Each rack bay may carry at most 2,700 kg. Free block-stacked pallets must not exceed 4.0 m in height and must not exceed 3 pallets high. The hazmat cage holds at most 3 full-weight pallets. Report any overweight, unstable or over-limit staging immediately.
7. PPE and truck safety. High-visibility vests are mandatory for everyone on the warehouse floor. Safety footwear class S3 is mandatory on the warehouse floor. Forklift operators must wear seat belts whenever the truck is moving.
8. Pre-use checks. Every forklift gets a documented pre-use check each day. The daily forklift check happens before first use each day in accordance with PR-10 Forklift and MHE operation. Do not operate a truck with open defects.
9. Sequence of warehouse tasks:
   a) Goods are booked into the system before put-away to storage under PR-02 Put-away to storage;
   b) Pick faces are replenished before the picking shift starts under PR-03 Replenishment of pick faces.
10. Pedestrian interaction. Comply with PR-11 Pedestrian safety in the warehouse, give way at crossings, sound horn at blind corners and maintain separation from the 1.2 m segregated pedestrian walkways.
11. Site standards. Racking gets a documented safety inspection every month. Keep aisles free of obstructions, respect bay load notices and park trucks in designated areas with forks lowered and brake applied.

